{
  "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
  "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:08 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Anders",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ANDERS: It's not an ordinary contract in that it doesn't give rise to contract rights in the -- in the regulated entities. This is very similar to Medicare provider agreements, in which a health care provider who wants to enter into the Medicare program and provide services agrees -- signs an agreement in which he agrees to abide by the statutes and regulations set forth in the Medicare program and, in return for that agreement, is given the opportunity to participate in the Medicaid -- in the Medicare program."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Is it -- is it a contract at all?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Anders",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ANDERS: That's exactly right. I think -- to take the first part of your question, I think the Court can tell when this is a regulatory contract when the statute itself simply directs the agency to enter into an agreement that -- that contains the terms that are set forth in the statute. And when you look at the statutory scheme as a whole, it is a regulatory scheme. The government is not acting as a contracting party here. It's acting as a regulator."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Anders",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ANDERS: We do agree with the Petitioners that -- that permitting third-party beneficiary suits here, if you construe this as a contract, would interfere with the government's ability to administer the statutory scheme."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Anders",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ANDERS: Well, in the covered entity context, the concern is that, because you have so many of them, if you -- if you start permitting covered entities to bring suit, you know, this is essentially a pre-emption question, but you then have 50 different State regimes, State court regimes, put onto -- grafted onto, the Medicaid rebate requirements."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So I understand your position clearly, in a regulatory contract situation like this one the Secretary is without authority to decide he or she can't enforce the statute and to confer expressly by contract third-party beneficiary rights to the -- to the people receiving the benefit? That's the position you're taking? If the Secretary had written a provision into this contract telling 340B entities you can sue, that would have been, according to you, ultra vires?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Frederick",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Number one, there's no evidence that Congress intended there to be a departure from normal operating contract principles, and this Court, in Winstar, in Mobil Oil, in Jackson Transit, in Central Airlines -- all said that when Congress uses contracts or agreements, it intends to incorporate the full cluster of the common law rights as they've existed. And third-party beneficiary rights have been recognized for 350 years, even before the founding of this republic."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, which way does that cut?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c648e57d9552f4318752f0dc00920f6c80e1c5d2456c70d62830740e56a66a26",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c648e57d9552-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What do you think about -- is the -- I'm not sure I'm right at all here, but as I understand the development of this argument today, it's open to you and the other 57 counties to go to the State AG, and you say you bring the lawsuit, okay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1273",
      "confidence": 0.0,
